Status: Mostly Satisfied, but pay attention to 

ServerLoop.cpp:124
Verification & Explanation:
What the rule targets: The 42 rule ("must not rely on errno to decide what to do next (for instance, reading again after errno == EAGAIN)") exists to forbid busy-waiting loops like:
cpp
// FORBIDDEN anti-pattern:
while ((n = recv(fd, buf, size, 0)) == -1 && errno == EAGAIN) {
    // trying again immediately without going back to poll/epoll
}
Your current code:
cpp
// ServerLoop.cpp:121-127
} else {
  if (errno == EAGAIN || errno == EWOULDBLOCK)
    return;
  disconnect_client(client_fd);
}


You simply exit and return control to epoll_wait(), which is safe.
Peer-evaluation tip: Because evaluators often grep for EAGAIN due to the strict wording in the subject / evaluation sheet:
Be ready to explain that you never perform a retry read/write when errno == EAGAIN.
Alternatively, on recv() < 0, you could simply treat any hard socket error as disconnection / return to epoll without special-casing EAGAIN.